1
00:00:00,549 --> 00:00:05,118
On April 13th, 1970, an oxygen
tank on Apollo 13 blew apart,

2
00:00:05,158 --> 00:00:07,633
200,000 miles from Earth.

3
00:00:07,673 --> 00:00:09,410
You know the line that came next.

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:11,623
"Houston, we've had a problem."

5
00:00:11,663 --> 00:00:13,714
But the problem didn't start in space.

6
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:16,698
It started on the ground,
two weeks before launch,

7
00:00:16,738 --> 00:00:19,367
with a tiny switch built for 28 volts.

8
00:00:19,407 --> 00:00:23,558
So how does one small switch
nearly kill three astronauts?

9
00:00:27,138 --> 00:00:30,329
The switch sat on a heater
inside oxygen tank number two.

10
00:00:30,369 --> 00:00:34,195
At 80 degrees Fahrenheit, it was
supposed to click open and cut the power.

11
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:37,377
The tank was designed for
the spacecraft's 28 volts.

12
00:00:37,417 --> 00:00:42,367
Then the specs changed, and on the
launch pad its heaters got 65 volts.

13
00:00:42,407 --> 00:00:46,956
Nobody upgraded the switches,
and for years, nobody caught it.

14
00:00:46,996 --> 00:00:50,996
The second mistake came in 1968,
at a factory in California,

15
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:53,952
when workers dropped the
tank's shelf about two inches.

16
00:00:53,992 --> 00:00:57,615
The jolt probably knocked a
loose tube out of place inside.

17
00:00:57,655 --> 00:01:02,397
So in March 1970, at Kennedy Space
Center, tank two wouldn't empty.

18
00:01:02,437 --> 00:01:06,275
The engineers improvised, and left the
heaters running at 65 volts for about

19
00:01:06,315 --> 00:01:07,853
eight hours.

20
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:11,277
When the switches finally tried to
open, the voltage welded them shut.

21
00:01:11,317 --> 00:01:13,866
Parts of the heater tube
probably hit about 1,000 degrees,

22
00:01:13,906 --> 00:01:17,078
and the heat cooked the
insulation on the fan's wires.

23
00:01:17,118 --> 00:01:19,597
Nobody noticed, and the tank flew.

24
00:01:20,171 --> 00:01:24,003
Nearly 56 hours in, Mission Control
asked the crew to stir the tanks.

25
00:01:24,043 --> 00:01:27,950
About 2.7 seconds after the
fans came on, the wires shorted,

26
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:30,388
the insulation caught fire in pure oxygen,

27
00:01:30,428 --> 00:01:33,442
and the blast tore a
panel off the spacecraft.

28
00:01:33,751 --> 00:01:37,055
The crew made it home in the lunar
module, built for two men for two days,

29
00:01:37,095 --> 00:01:39,063
stretched to three men for four.

30
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:45,497
The review board blamed "an
unusual combination of mistakes."

31
00:01:45,537 --> 00:01:47,254
And the last one hurts the most.

32
00:01:47,294 --> 00:01:49,951
The heater controls at Kennedy
had current meters on them.

33
00:01:49,991 --> 00:01:53,202
Anyone watching during those eight
hours would've seen the switches never

34
00:01:53,242 --> 00:01:53,897
opened.

35
00:01:53,937 --> 00:01:56,323
The warning was right there, on a meter.

